          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have been on a proton pump inhibitor for six years. Is long-term use a concern, what does guidance describe about review, and how is reflux coded?",
          "answer": "Regarding concerns with long-term use of proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) and guidance for review, the provided information does not specifically detail potential concerns or review guidance for long-term PPI use (consultation). Clinical guidelines also do not specify a routine review process for long-term PPI therapy (research).\n\nHowever, one guideline notes that long-term use of certain medications is associated with lower vitamin B12 levels, and therefore, periodic measurement is advised (National diabetes association standards of care). While this statement appears in the context of type 2 diabetes medication, it provides a general concern for long-term use that could be discussed with a clinician (research).\n\nFor adults experiencing typical symptoms of heartburn and regurgitation without any alarm features, clinical guidance suggests an initial empiric eight-week trial of a PPI taken before a meal (research).\n\nThe ICD-10 code for gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is K21 (coding-gerd-k21). This code distinguishes between reflux disease with esophagitis and reflux disease without esophagitis (coding-gerd-k21). Reflux disease without esophagitis is commonly used, as the distinction relies on endoscopic findings that may not always be documented (coding-gerd-k21). Esophagitis refers to a finding on the lining of the esophagus, not the severity of symptoms (coding-gerd-k21).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
          "wall_ms": 30118.614468000033,
